    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d445">

                <pb facs="1135" n="1091" sameAs="frus:frus1969-76v29/pg1091"/>

                <head>445. Memorandum From President Nixon to his Assistant for National Security Affairs (Kissinger)<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d445-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1971-04-27">April 27, 1971</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Moynihanʼs idea of buying the entire Turkish opium crowd is one that seems
                    intriguing. I realize there are problems here, but would you have a check made
                    to see what, if anything, could be done. Connally seems to like the idea, provided it does not have other
                    foreign policy implications which would militate against it.<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d445-f2"/>
                </p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d445-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d445-f1-content">
                <p>Source: National Archives, Nixon
                    Presidential Materials, NSC Files, Subject
                    Files, Box 341, HAK/President Memos 1971–. No
                    classification marking.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d445-f2" n="2" xml:id="d445-f2-content">
                <p>The idea was endorsed by Secretary of the Treasury Connally during an April 27 meeting with the President.
                    (Memorandum from Haig to Sonnenfeldt and Saunders, April 28; ibid.)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
